Short:        MSX/MSX2/MSX2+ emulator, accuracy update
Author:       Juan Antonio Gomez Galvez (GUS Entertainment / RED Soft)
Uploader:     Zoopster (JCEC_1@yahoo.co.uk)
Type:         misc/emu
Version:      4.0
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0.0
Requires:     68030 or better, AGA
Distribution: Aminet
Kurz:         MSX-Emulator (MSX2/2+), mehr Genauigkeit

AmiMSX 4.0 is a community update of AmiMSX, the MSX, MSX2 and MSX2+
emulator by Juan Antonio Gomez Galvez (GUS Entertainment / RED Soft),
released with his permission. It is built from his v3.1 source, with parts
of his later v3.2/v3.5 source. This version puts accuracy first: many
games that broke or glitched in 3.x now run as on a real MSX.

As before, AmiMSX takes over the machine while the MSX runs and drives the
custom chips directly. HELP returns to the menu.

Requirements
------------
- AmigaOS 3.0 or later.
- A 68030 or better. The low-spec target is an A1200 or similar with a
  68030 at 50 MHz and fast RAM. A stock 68020 A1200 runs it, but slowly.
- AGA. On ECS AmiMSX warns that it needs AGA and can carry on, with
  limits, as 3.x did.
- The libraries and devices listed in AmiMSX.guide (as for 3.1).
- PiStorm (Emu68), BigMig or another fast CPU for MSX-Music (FM), 60 Hz
  games at full speed and Smooth display.

Installation
------------
Copy the AmiMSX drawer anywhere and start one of the preset icons:

  AmiMSX 030      68030/50 with fast RAM: PSG, PAL, sprites every frame
  AmiMSX PiStorm  PiStorm, BigMig and other fast CPUs: PSG+FM, display
                  frequency from the VDP, Japanese ID (60 Hz), audio
                  filter, smooth display

Each preset keeps its own settings file (ToolType PREFS=<file>), and Save
Prefs in the menu writes to the preset you started.

AmiMSX4.romcache is created next to the program on first use: each
MegaROM is hashed once and its mapper looked up in AmiMSX4.romdb. Leave
the drawer writable.

The included AmiMSX2.data is the original one (MSX, MSX2 and MSX2+ system
ROMs, disk ROM and Kanji), as in the earlier AmiMSX packages, so disk
images and MSX-BASIC work.

What's new
----------
- Z80: POP/RET through ROM, RLD/RRD on ROM, NEG/CPL flags, M1 wait
  states, LD A,R from emulated time.
- VDP: sprite collisions, the 5th sprite flag, sprite mode 2 bands and
  mid-frame splits (sprite tables, SCREEN 4 tables, SCREEN 5/4 modes,
  palettes, R#8/R#18/R#23, display off), transparent copies, LMMC.
- MSX2+: the machine is back, with V9958 fine scroll (R#27) and the left
  mask (R#25) per line (Space Manbow, Nemesis 3 Enhanced, Chikubi Ninja).
- Sound: MSX-Music (FM) on Paula channel 3 ("PSG+FM", or "FM lite" with
  the 4 loudest voices); PSG volume in 3 dB steps as the YM2149.
- Display: double-buffered sprites, Smooth display (SCREEN 4) for fast
  CPUs, automatic edge mask for games that scroll sideways.
- Options: Use Japanese ID (60 Hz), Audio filter, Mask screen edges
  (always or auto), per-game settings next to each cartridge.
- Cartridges: ROM database (mapper by SHA-1), 8 KB, 48/64 KB and 4 MB
  ROMs, flash Konami SCC cartridges with saves (Pampas & Selene).
- Disks: drive B with its own image (written back), disk swap with Left
  Amiga.
- Freeware texts and no anti-crack counter, from Juan's v3.2/v3.5.
- Cleaner exits when a screen, window or device can't be opened.

See Docs/CHANGES_4.0.md for the full list.

Known limits
------------
- Smooth display covers SCREEN 4 only; other modes run as before.
- MSX2+: fine scroll and the left mask only. No coarse scroll (R#26),
  two-page scroll (SP2) or YJK modes (SCREEN 10-12) yet, and no fine
  scroll in SCREEN 2 (Salamander Enhanced).
- Jailbreak: scenery rows flash at the top of the playfield (bitmap
  splits are timed from the Amiga beam).
- Head over Heels Remake: letters missing from the menu text.
- Nemesis 2: stray cyan dots on the star field.
- Barbarian: the sky is lighter than on an MSX.
- Bombaman: moving characters look ghosted on fast machines (seen on
  PiStorm and BigMig), though each frame is correct.
- Manbow 2: the intro screens are drawn corrupted, and in play the
  ship jumps back 3-4 dots now and then while it moves.
- Lilly's Saga: on fast machines the save-slot menu text can be drawn
  as solid blocks (seen with Smooth display off).
- CD32-style pads (e.g. KTRL CD32): a direction and the fire button
  can't be held together in some games (Pampas & Selene); a plain
  joystick works.

